Weekly Performance Report — D2C Subscription Brand

End-to-end weekly analytics system for a subscription-based D2C brand within a multi-brand e-commerce portfolio. Owns the full lifecycle from data extraction to executive decision-making.

Context

A subscription-based consumer brand operating in the US, with acquisition driven by paid media (Meta, AppLovin, Google Search, Google Shopping) and retention driven by email, SMS, and recurring revenue streams.

Within a multi-brand portfolio, marketing budgets are dynamically allocated across brands, making weekly performance reporting a critical input for capital allocation decisions.

Problem

Before this system existed, weekly performance tracking was:

Fragmented across ad platforms, CRM tools, and warehouse queries
Inconsistent in metric definitions across teams
Too slow for weekly budget reallocation decisions

As a result, leadership lacked a single source of truth to answer:

Are we on track vs plan (weekly, MTD, YoY)?
Where is performance coming from (paid vs non-paid, channel vs product)?
What changed WoW that requires action?

This led to delayed decisions and inefficient budget allocation across channels.

Solution

Designed and delivered a unified weekly reporting system structured around executive decision-making.

1. Executive One-Pager

Concise narrative summarizing:

Spend and revenue performance
Blended vs paid ROAS
YoY and MTD performance vs plan
Key drivers of WoW changes
Required actions for the next week
2. Performance Overview
Daily trends for spend, revenue, and ROAS
Channel contribution breakdown
Paid vs non-paid revenue split
3. Channel Deep Dives

Granular analysis per acquisition channel and product line, including:

ROAS, CPA, CPM, CTR, CPC
Funnel metrics (ATC → Checkout → Purchase)
Revenue per visitor
AOV and conversion efficiency
4. Retention & CRM Performance

Email & SMS performance segmented by:

Flow (Welcome, Abandoned Cart, etc.)
Broadcast type (Acquisition vs Retention)

Metrics include CTR, CVR, revenue per send, and unsubscribe rates.

5. YoY & Plan Reconciliation
Trailing week YoY comparison
Month-to-date vs plan tracking
Channel-level variance decomposition
Data & Technical Implementation
Data Layer

SQL-based extraction from internal warehouse (Presto / Athena), joining:

Order-level transactions
Subscription lifecycle data
Marketing attribution tables
Processing Layer

Python pipeline (Pandas / NumPy):

Data cleaning and standardization
WoW and YoY calculations
Cross-source metric validation
Export of structured datasets for reporting
Reporting Layer

Automated slide generation using standardized templates to ensure:

Consistent weekly comparability
Fast executive consumption (<5 min reading time)
Stable visual hierarchy for trend recognition
Data Quality & Governance

Implemented strict validation rules:

Dual-source spend reconciliation across platforms
Hard separation between acquisition vs retention revenue
Standardized metric definitions across tools
No speculative interpretation allowed in reporting narrative
